<% content_for :title, 'Commerce' %>

<%= render 'help/page', title: 'Commerce' do %>
  <div class='space-y-8'>

    <p class='text-fg-muted'>
      Generates traffic between two star systems. Choose an origin and destination once, then
      switch between the Passage, Freight, Mail, and Speculative Trade tabs — each keeps its own
      inputs and results. A map of the route is shown alongside the tabs when the two systems are
      close enough together to fit on one.
    </p>

    <div>
      <dl class='space-y-6'>
        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>From / To</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Origin and destination star systems, chosen by search. Shared by every tab. Each
            system's UWP, trade codes, and travel zone are shown beneath the relevant field on
            every tab, alongside the individual DMs that field contributes to that tab's result.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class='text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-highlight mb-4'>Passage</p>

      <dl class='space-y-6'>
        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Skill Effect / Steward / Other DM</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Manual inputs feeding the passenger calculation. Defaults for these can be set
            campaign-wide in <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>Settings → Campaign → Passenger
            Traffic</span>.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Departure Board</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Generates passenger traffic and shows a Low/Basic/Middle/High card for each passage
            class.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Passenger cards</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Each card shows the number of passengers found, the total DM applied and its
            individual components, and the qualifying and count results behind the total. Counts
            are not capped to a world's population, so the UWP shown beside each system is there
            for the referee to judge plausibility against.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class='text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-highlight mb-4'>Freight</p>

      <dl class='space-y-6'>
        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Skill Effect / Other DM</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Manual inputs feeding the freight calculation. Defaults for these can be set
            campaign-wide in <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>Settings → Campaign → Freight
            Traffic</span>.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Cargo Manifest</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Generates freight traffic and shows an Incidental/Minor/Major card for each cargo lot
            size.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Lot cards</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Each card shows the number of lots found, the total tonnage, the total DM applied and
            its individual components, and the results behind the total.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class='text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-highlight mb-4'>Mail</p>

      <dl class='space-y-6'>
        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Ship Armed / Naval-Scout Rank / SOC DM / Other DM</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Manual inputs feeding the mail calculation, alongside the Freight DM already computed
            for the same route on the Freight tab. Defaults for these can be set campaign-wide in
            <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>Settings → Campaign → Mail</span>.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Mail Manifest</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Determines whether mail is available and shows the result — how many containers were
            offered and the values and DMs behind it.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class='text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-highlight mb-4'>Speculative Trade</p>

      <dl class='space-y-6'>
        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Re-Survey Market</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Generates a fresh Goods Available list at the origin world. The list otherwise stays
            stable across page reloads — only this button generates a new one.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Goods Available</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            The origin world's generated goods list, shown as a table of Good, Category, Tons, and
            Base Price. Population and trade codes used to build the list are shown above the
            table.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Skill Effect, Broker Skill, Other DM</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Manual inputs applied to every good in one pass — <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>Skill
            Effect</span> is the Traveller's own Broker check, <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>Broker
            Skill</span> is the counterpart's (the supplier's when purchasing, the buyer's when
            selling), and <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>Other DM</span> covers any further
            situational factor. Purchase and Sale each have their own set of these fields.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Local Broker</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Toggle to hire a local broker instead of using the Traveller's own Skill Effect. While
            on, <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>Skill Effect</span> is replaced by
            <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>Level</span> and <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>Fee
            %</span> fields, pre-filled from the campaign's defaults but editable per transaction.
            Defaults can be set campaign-wide in
            <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>Settings → Campaign → Speculative Trade</span>.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Negotiate Purchase Prices / Negotiate Sale Prices</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Generates a price for every good and adds a Purchase Price (or Sale Price) column to
            its table. Purchase prices apply to the Goods Available table; Sale generates a price
            for every priceable good in its own table, since the Travellers may be selling cargo
            bought anywhere, not only goods found at the origin.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Illegal goods and Exotics</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            This tool does not model finding a supplier or a black market, so illegal goods are
            generated and priced the same as any other good — the referee should judge legality,
            smuggling consequences, and Law Level narratively. Exotics are shown as a find with no
            tons or price.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Referee-tunable Base Prices</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Only Base Price is campaign-overridable — availability, tons, and Purchase/Sale DMs are
            fixed sourcebook data. Override prices under
            <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>Data Cores → Trade Goods</span>, not Campaign
            Settings.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class='pt-2 text-right uppercase font-mono text-xs tracking-[0.22em] text-fg-muted'>
      REFERENCE 0810CM
    </div>

  </div>
<% end %>
